<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Euboean League</h1><p class="st-meta">411 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.) · capital Chalcis</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Federation of the cities of Euboea, born from the island's revolt against Athens in 411 BCE and revived repeatedly under Macedonian and Roman patrons.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a> was vital to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a>, supplying food and pasture. In 411 BCE, after a Spartan naval victory off <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eretria">Eretria</a>, its cities revolted and formed a league, building a bridge across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euripus-strait">Euripus</a> to link <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chalcis">Chalcis</a> with the mainland. Federal coins inscribed for the Euboeans appear from about this time.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The league joined <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chalcis">Chalcis</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eretria">Eretria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/histiaea">Histiaea</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/karystos">Carystus</a> with a federal council and coinage, though the larger cities often pursued their own policies. Its history was one of shifting allegiances: members joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/second-athenian-league">Second Athenian League</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callias-of-chalcis">Callias of Chalcis</a> revived the federation in the 340s BCE in a contest between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II</a> for the island.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the Hellenistic kings, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chalcis">Chalcis</a> was one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon's</a> 'fetters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>'. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-quinctius-flamininus">Flamininus</a> proclaimed the freedom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> in 196 BCE the league was refounded under Roman patronage. It lost its independence after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaean-war">Achaean War</a> of 146 BCE, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> was punished for supporting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaeans-achaea">Achaeans</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/euboean-league.jpg" alt="Euboean League" width="320" height="154"><figcaption>Image: CNG, (uploaded by Odysses) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Euboea_drachma.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>411 BCE – 146 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chalcis</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Callias of Chalcis</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek (Ionic)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Koinon of the Euboeans</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-278&amp;focus=euboean-league" aria-label="Euboean League on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5266 797 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Euboean League lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5586 1053L5586 1053M5586 1053L5592 1055M5586 1053L5552 978"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5586 1053h0M5592 1055h0M5552 978h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5586 1053h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5586 1053h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Chalcis. <a href="/atlas?year=-278&amp;focus=euboean-league">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

